A||||6||XDrewX||87-88 / headlight swap to an 86||||1||
Z||000000||XDrewX||11-25-2001||12:20 AM||||Can you swap the newer style 87-88 headlight assembly, (motor/light bracket assmbly), to an 85 or 86 car.<P>When I mean swap, I am talking about unplugging the motor wire and the lamp wire, remove the 4 10mm nuts, take entire assembly out of 87 car, put into 85 car, and plug back in, put nuts back on and your done, kind of swap.<P>Thanks, <P>Andrew||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||11-25-2001||12:55 AM||||Alittle more to it than that. Check out Ogre's cave....in the chime section on the index section on the left.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My7logo_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-25-2001||12:58 AM||||The lamp lift assembly is the same, however the lift motor is not and can not be made compatible with the old system.<P>To use the 87 motor you need it's wiring and the motor controller.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Smoooooth GT||11-25-2001||12:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by XDrewX:<BR><B>Can you swap the newer style 87-88 headlight assembly, (motor/light bracket assmbly), to an 85 or 86 car.<P>When I mean swap, I am talking about unplugging the motor wire and the lamp wire, remove the 4 10mm nuts, take entire assembly out of 87 car, put into 85 car, and plug back in, put nuts back on and your done, kind of swap.<P>Thanks, <P>Andrew</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well... You need to swap motor's of course.. Then you need the 87-88 controller that's located behind the driver's side front fascia bolted to the body( this is also where you get your power for your horn,etc.) and you also need a 87-88 wiring harness that's bolted to the front firewall just under your brake booster. Like My7Fiero's said.. If you have further question's.. Look in theorge's cave under chime box... Oh, I almost forgot to tell you... The cave has been looked through and it's *safe*... No Taliban, or Osama!!! Good Luck... Ley me know how it goes, Because I'm fixing to have to do this same thing to the Smoooooth GT!!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Smooooth_Yellow_sig_3.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/texas_1.gif">          <BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-25-2001||02:14 PM||||Uh guys.... there's nothing about the headlights in the chime unit article in my cave. There is a link to <A HREF="http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/index.html" TARGET=_blank>the michigan club</A> and they do have info on altering the old OE harness for gen 2 light motors.<P>There is a headlight article but it's mainly about replacing/aiming headlights and why I like Philips HV lamps.<P>The best way to convert the system, if you can get all the parts, is swap the front wiring harness but that works IF and only IF you have the single speed radiator fan. (And no, you don't have to put the light module under the fender.)<P>If you have the 2 speed cooling fan then Michigan's method is best.<P>If you do the Michigan club's method, you can use parts from Sunbird and other late 80's and up GM cars altho you may have to work out some wire colors....||||reg||1
Z||000005||XDrewX||11-25-2001||02:32 PM||||Thanks for everyones help, but I think I will leave well enough alone and just buy a rebuild kit from Rodney.<P>I would like another opinion on this subject, Here are the factors:<P>1. I have two perfect(rebuilt with new rollers) 87 light assembles complete. from a 50k mile car.<P>2. My 86 lights need the gear replaced, but If I am going to mess with it, I am going to do a complete rebuild with everthing Rodney has for 86 Lights.<P>I am looking for what would be more reliable in the long run.<P>  Are the 87/88 motors THAT much better ?, that it would be worth while to cut and splice,etc.<P>Cost is not a factor, I want to fix these lights one time and have them last for a long time.  <BR>Note: the 86 Motors have about 100k on them.<P>I bought both cars used, and really have no clue whic lights have been used more, except that the 87 lights still have the original light in it and it is crystal clear with no scratches etc.  <BR>The 86 light is a replacement.<P>Thanks, <P>Andrew||||reg||1
Z||000006||Matthew_Fiero||11-25-2001||02:36 PM||||i did the conversion last week<BR>it is worth it in the long run because it is expensive to rebuilt the 84-86 style motors, and the 87-88 style is much quieter and faster<BR>i just swapped wiring and motors<BR>||||reg||1
